Below is a **self‑contained console application** that shows the most common ways to work with *nullable value types* (`Nullable<T>`), which in C# are written as `T?`.  
Feel free to copy‑paste the code into a new `dotnet new console` project and run it.

```csharp
using System;

namespace NullableDemo
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // 1. Declaring a nullable value type (int? = Nullable<int>)
            int? nullableInt = null;          // no value yet

            // 2. Checking if it has a value
            Console.WriteLine("HasValue? " + nullableInt.HasValue);

            // 3. Accessing the value safely
            //    - throws InvalidOperationException if no value
            //    - the safest approach is to test HasValue first
            if (nullableInt.HasValue)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Value: {nullableInt.Value}");
            }
            else
            {
                Console.WriteLine("No value yet.");
            }

            // 4. Assign a real value
            nullableInt = 42;
            Console.WriteLine($"After assignment: {nullableInt} (HasValue = {nullableInt.HasValue})");

            // 5. Null‑coalescing operator (??) – provides a default when null
            int defaulted = nullableInt ?? -1;   // will be 42
            Console.WriteLine($"Defaulted value: {defaulted}");

            int? nullInt = null;
            int defaultedFromNull = nullInt ?? 99; // will be 99
            Console.WriteLine($"Defaulted from null: {defaultedFromNull}");

            // 6. Pattern matching with nullable types
            if (nullableInt is int value)   // unpacks the value if present
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Pattern matched value: {value}");
            }

            // 7. Using nullable in a method that returns a nullable result
            int? result = TryParseInt("123");
            Console.WriteLine($"TryParseInt(\"123\") = {result}");

            result = TryParseInt("abc");
            Console.WriteLine($"TryParseInt(\"abc\") = {result}");
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Attempts to parse an integer from the string.  Returns null if parsing fails.
        /// </summary>
        static int? TryParseInt(string s)
        {
            if (int.TryParse(s, out int value))
                return value;      // boxed into Nullable<int>
            else
                return null;       // represents the absence of a value
        }
    }
}
```

### What the code demonstrates

| Feature | Example |
|---------|---------|
| **Declaring a nullable value type** | `int? nullableInt = null;` |
| **Checking for a value** | `nullableInt.HasValue` |
| **Accessing the value** | `nullableInt.Value` (only after `HasValue` is `true`) |
| **Null‑coalescing operator** | `int result = nullableInt ?? -1;` |
| **Pattern matching** | `if (nullableInt is int value) …` |
| **Returning nullable from a method** | `int? TryParseInt(string s)` |

### A quick note on nullable *reference* types

Starting with C# 8.0 you can also enable *nullable reference types* (`string?`, `object?`, etc.) which are a compile‑time feature, not a runtime one.  
To enable them, add `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>` to your project file, and then you’ll see compiler warnings if you dereference a possibly‑null reference. The concepts shown above for value types are very similar in spirit.

---

Run the program and you’ll see output that confirms each step works as expected. Feel free to modify the values and try different scenarios to see how nullable types behave in practice!
